NPKernel opt-in ring-3 verification
Date: 2026-08-16

The isolated diagnostic build used NPK_ENABLE_USER_DEMO=1 and booted with:
qemu-system-x86_64 -M pc -m 256M -cdrom build/npkernel.iso -drive if=ide,format=raw,file=build/npkernel-disk.img -serial file:build/ring3-fix.log -debugcon file:build/ring3-fix-debugcon.log -global isa-debugcon.iobase=0xe9 -display none -no-reboot -no-shutdown

Observed serial markers:
[INFO] proc: exec address-space root
[INFO] elf: secure PT_LOAD pages
[INFO] proc: exec loaded entry
       value=0x0000000000400000
[INFO] proc: created user pid
       value=0x0000000000000002
[INFO] user: launching ring3 pid
       value=0x0000000000000002
S[INFO] syscall: user syscall number
       value=0x000000000000003c
[INFO] proc: process exited
       value=0x0000000000000002
[INFO] syscall: exit syscall completed; yielding to scheduler
[INFO] sched: kernel thread executed
       value=0x0000005343484544

Result: PASS. The validated hello.elf entered ring 3 at 0x400000, reached the first instruction of syscall_entry, completed Linux x86_64 syscall number 60, marked the process exited, and transferred control to the cooperative kernel thread. The diagnostic run contained no page fault, general-protection fault, or panic marker. The one-shot demo masks legacy PIC IRQ0/IRQ1 immediately before entry because full user-mode interrupt scheduling remains future work.

The production source and final default image use NPK_ENABLE_USER_DEMO=0. Temporary S/F debugcon and serial markers were removed after the isolated test.
